        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Crécy</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">26 August 1346, with the fighting continuing late into the night and a further clash the next morning</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">A sloping hillside at Crécy-en-Ponthieu, with Wadicourt on the English left and Crécy and the River Maye on the right, in Ponthieu in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> under King Edward III, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> and the earls of Northampton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a> and Arundel, perhaps 7,000 to 15,000 men by modern estimates; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under King Philip VI, with Charles of Alençon, Rudolph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Louis of Blois, by consensus substantially larger, at least twice and perhaps three times the English by Clifford Rogers's estimate</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The English held their position through repeated French attacks and suffered very few losses, while the French lost heavily, among them King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and Count Charles of Alençon. Philip left the field near midnight.</span></div>
        </div>
