    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The English chose and prepared the ground: a sloping hillside with its flanks protected, pits dug in front, dismounted men-at-arms in the centre and longbowmen on the flanks, so that the French had to charge uphill.</li><li>Estimates of the French army run from about 72,000 or 120,000 in two chronicles to a modern view of at least twice and perhaps three times the English, and the same sources differ on the English as well, from 7,000 to 15,000.</li><li>The English laid siege to Calais, which fell eleven months later and stayed in English hands for two hundred years, and the battle established the longbow as a dominant weapon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Each English archer was issued two more quivers on the morning of the battle, making 72 arrows per man, and modern historians suggest that half a million arrows could have been shot.</p></aside>
    </section>

