    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1337</span><span class="rf-text">Philip VI's Great Council takes back the lands Edward III holds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which marks the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1346</span><span class="rf-text">A French army under John, Duke of Normandy, marches on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> and besieges Aiguillon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1346</span><span class="rf-text">The English land at Saint-Vaast-la-Hougue in Normandy and march south, storming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> on 26 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1346</span><span class="rf-text">The English reach within 2 miles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, then turn north and cross the Somme at the ford of Blanchetaque</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late August 1346</span><span class="rf-text">The French attack the English position at Crécy-en-Ponthieu, and the Genoese crossbowmen are driven off by the longbowmen</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late August 1346</span><span class="rf-text">French cavalry charges are broken until nearly midnight, and Philip leaves the field</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late August 1346</span><span class="rf-text">French forces still arriving are charged by the English, now mounted, and pursued for miles</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1347</span><span class="rf-text">Calais falls to the English after a siege of eleven months</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-crecy" data-v="d31e45783b" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The English hold the hillside</b> Edward's army stood in three battles on a slope, with pits dug in front. French scouts came in sight of them from Abbeville, and the French column followed with the Genoese crossbowmen in front.</li><li><b>The archery duel</b> The Genoese went forward and shot at the English longbowmen. The longbowmen outranged them and shot more than three times as fast, and the crossbowmen had no pavises, which were still with the French baggage.</li><li><b>The Genoese flee through their own cavalry</b> The Italians were quickly defeated and fled. Knights in Alençon's division, hampered by the routed mercenaries, hacked at them as they retreated, and the clash threw the leading battle into disarray.</li><li><b>Alençon's charge uphill</b> Alençon's battle charged, disordered by its hurried start, the fleeing Italians, the mud, the slope and the pits. Archers shot the unarmoured horses, and the charge had lost much of its force when it reached the English men-at-arms.</li><li><b>The first charge is beaten off</b> The English men-at-arms and spearmen received a charge that had lost much of its force, and the hand-to-hand fighting that followed was fierce. Alençon was among those killed, and the French attack was beaten off.</li><li><b>Fresh French cavalry repeat the charge</b> A fresh body of French cavalry moved up to the foot of the hill and charged over ground littered with dead and wounded men and horses. They were too disordered to break into the English formation, and a long mêlée followed.</li><li><b>Edward sends a detachment from the reserve</b> With the Prince of Wales hard pressed, Edward sent forward a detachment from his reserve battle to rescue the situation, and the French were repulsed again.</li><li><b>The charges go on into the night</b> The French repeated their charges late into the night, each ending in fierce fighting and a French retreat. The English ranks were thinned, but men from the rear stepped forward to fill the gaps.</li><li><b>Philip leaves the field</b> Philip was caught up in the fighting, had two horses killed under him and was hit by an arrow in the jaw. He left the field, though it is unclear why, and near midnight most of the French army melted away.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war.jpg" alt="Hundred Years' War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_von_Azincourt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066, English monarchs had held titles and lands within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which made them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the kings of France. After a series of disagreements between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, Philip's Great Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> agreed on 24 May 1337 that the lands Edward held in France should be taken back into Philip's hands, on the grounds that Edward was in breach of his obligations as a vassal. This marked the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, which was to last 116 years.</p><p>Eight years of intermittent, expensive and inconclusive warfare followed. Edward campaigned three times in northern France to no effect, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> was left almost entirely to its own devices. In 1345 Henry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a>, led a campaign through Gascony, defeated two large French armies at Bergerac and Auberoche and captured more than 100 towns and fortifications. In March 1346 a French army of between 15,000 and 20,000 under John, Duke of Normandy, the son and heir of Philip VI, marched on Gascony and besieged the town of Aiguillon.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a>, now Lancaster, sent an urgent appeal for help to Edward, who was bound by his indenture with Lancaster to rescue him if he were attacked by overwhelming numbers. Edward raised a fresh army and assembled more than 700 vessels to carry it, the largest English fleet to that date. The French relied on their navy to guard northern France, and that reliance proved misplaced.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Crécy&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Jonathan Sumption, The Hundred Years War 1: Trial by Battle (Faber &amp; Faber, 1990)</li><li>Michael Prestwich, Plantagenet England 1225–1360 (Oxford University Press, 2007)</li><li>John A. Wagner, Encyclopedia of the Hundred Years War (Greenwood Press, 2006)</li><li>Marilyn Livingstone, The Road to Crécy: The English Invasion of France, 1346 (Routledge, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The English army was made up almost exclusively of English and Welsh soldiers, with a handful of disaffected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> and a few German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, probably no more than 150 foreigners in all. Its exact size and make-up are not known. Modern historians have estimated it at from 7,000 to 15,000. Andrew Ayton suggests around 14,000, Clifford Rogers 15,000, and Jonathan Sumption, going by the carrying capacity of the transport fleet, 7,000 to 10,000. Up to a thousand men were convicted felons serving on the promise of a pardon. The men-at-arms fought dismounted, and the army also had small guns, ribauldequins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombard">bombards</a> in unknown numbers.</p><p>Edward drew up three battles in a column on a sloping hillside facing south-east. The Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, aided by the earls of Northampton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a>, commanded the vanguard with 800 men-at-arms, 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and 1,000 foot soldiers. The Earl of Arundel led a second battle with 800 men-at-arms and 1,200 archers, and the King held the reserve with 700 men-at-arms and 2,000 archers. The flanks were secured by Wadicourt, Crécy and the River Maye, pits were dug in front, and the baggage was circled behind.</p><p>The size of the French army is even less certain. The two chroniclers who give totals say 72,000 or 120,000, figures historians call exaggerated. Rogers estimates that it was at least twice as large as the English and perhaps three times. By modern estimates 8,000 mounted men-at-arms formed its core, with two to six thousand Genoese crossbowmen and a large, indeterminate number of common infantry. The Genoese, under Antonio Doria and Carlo Grimaldi, formed the vanguard, followed by Charles of Alençon with King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, then Rudolph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Louis of Blois, with Philip commanding the rearguard.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Crécy&#8221; cites where it covers English army, French army, Initial deployments:</p><ul><li>Kelly DeVries, Infantry Warfare in the Early Fourteenth Century (Boydell Press, 1998)</li><li>Marilyn Livingstone, The Road to Crécy: The English Invasion of France, 1346 (Routledge, 2004)</li><li>Jonathan Sumption, The Hundred Years War 1: Trial by Battle (Faber &amp; Faber, 1990)</li><li>Clifford Rogers, War Cruel and Sharp: English Strategy under Edward III, 1327–1360 (Boydell Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Around noon on 26 August French scouts came in sight of the English. A council of war advised attacking the next day, as the army was tired from a 12-mile march, but the French attacked later the same afternoon. It is unclear whether this was Philip's choice or whether too many knights pressed forward. His plan was for the crossbowmen to disorder the English infantry so that the mounted men-at-arms could break in. As the French advanced with the oriflamme unfurled, a sudden rainstorm broke. The English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> unstrung their bows. Some historians hold that the rain weakened the Genoese strings, others that leather coverings protected them.</p><p>The longbowmen outranged the crossbowmen and shot more than three times as fast, and the Genoese were without their pavises, which were still with the baggage. The Genoese were rapidly defeated and fled. French knights following them hacked at them as they retreated, and the leading battle fell into disarray. Alençon's battle then charged, disordered by the fleeing Italians, the mud, the uphill slope and the pits, and broken up further by the English archers, whose arrows killed or wounded the unarmoured horses in large numbers.</p><p>Fresh French cavalry repeated the charge over ground littered with dead and wounded horses and men. The Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> was reported beaten to his knees at one point, and Edward sent help from his reserve. How many times the French charged is disputed, but the attacks continued late into the night, each ending in fierce fighting and a French retreat. Blind King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> tied his bridle to those of his attendants and rode into the twilight, and all were killed. Philip had two horses killed under him, took an arrow in the jaw and left the field near midnight.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Crécy&#8221; cites where it covers Archery duel, Cavalry charges:</p><ul><li>Kelly DeVries, Infantry Warfare in the Early Fourteenth Century (Boydell Press, 1998)</li><li>Michael Prestwich, Plantagenet England 1225–1360 (Oxford University Press, 2007)</li><li>Marilyn Livingstone, The Road to Crécy: The English Invasion of France, 1346 (Routledge, 2004)</li><li>Jonathan Sumption, The Hundred Years War 1: Trial by Battle (Faber &amp; Faber, 1990)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The English slept where they had fought. The next morning substantial French forces were still arriving. The English men-at-arms, now mounted, charged them and pursued them for miles, and the losses in that clash were reported as several thousand, including the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Estimates of the dead on the second day run from 2,000 to, according to Edward III himself, 4,000. Some sources say Edward ordered that no prisoners be taken, but there is no record of any until the next day.</p><p>All contemporary sources agree that English losses were very low. Three or four men-at-arms and a small number of the rank and file were reported dead, forty in all by a roll-call after the battle, though some modern historians think it too few and put the English dead at around three hundred. English heralds counted the bodies of 1,542 French noble men-at-arms, perhaps not including those who died the next day. The dead included one king, nine princes, ten counts, a duke, an archbishop and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><p>No reliable figures exist for the common soldiers. Jean Le Bel estimated 15,000 to 16,000 French dead and Froissart 30,000 killed or captured, while Alfred Burne guessed 10,000 infantry, for a total of 12,000 French dead. Edward then laid siege to Calais, which fell after eleven months, the battle having crippled the French army's ability to relieve it. Duke John had left Aiguillon on 20 August but did not arrive in time to change the course of events in the north.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Crécy&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Jonathan Sumption, The Hundred Years War 1: Trial by Battle (Faber &amp; Faber, 1990)</li><li>Clifford Rogers, War Cruel and Sharp: English Strategy under Edward III, 1327–1360 (Boydell Press, 2000)</li><li>Kelly DeVries, Infantry Warfare in the Early Fourteenth Century (Boydell Press, 1998)</li><li>John A. Wagner, Encyclopedia of the Hundred Years War (Greenwood Press, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from chroniclers writing in its time. Froissart wrote more than one version of his Chronicles, and his estimates of the English army differ so much between them that the third more than doubles the first. Jean Le Bel gives a figure for the French dead, an Italian chronicler gives his own totals for the French army, and the account of the hand-to-hand fighting as murderous and without pity comes from a contemporary. The financial records of the French campaign are lost, which is why the size of the French army cannot be fixed. English heralds counted the noble dead, and iron balls compatible with bombard ammunition have been recovered from the site.</p><p>Much is disputed. Historians differ on the size of both armies, on whether the rain weakened the Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a>, on how many casualties the Genoese suffered, on whether Edward gave an order to take no prisoners, on how many times the French charged, on how many English died, and on why Philip left the field. The sources also leave unclear whether the French attack was Philip's deliberate choice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Clifford Rogers calls the result a total victory for the English, and Andrew Ayton calls it unprecedented and a devastating military humiliation for the French. Jonathan Sumption considers it a political catastrophe for the French Crown. The battle was reported to the English parliament on 13 September in glowing terms, as a sign of divine favour and a justification for the huge cost of the war to date. Rogers writes that the English benefitted from superior organisation, cohesion and leadership and from the indiscipline of the French. Ayton writes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s international reputation as a military power was established in an evening's hard fighting.</p><p>Calais gave the English a port and a foothold in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that they held until 1558, more than two centuries. The battle established the effectiveness of the longbow as a dominant weapon on the Western European battlefield, and English and Welsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in significant numbers and some as far afield as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The historian Joseph Dahmus includes Crécy in his Seven Decisive Battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Crécy on 26 August 1346 Edward III's English army, drawn up on a prepared hillside, beat off repeated attacks by a much larger French army under Philip VI and lost very few men. The English then took Calais, which they held until 1558.</em></p>

